# results/runs.jsonl is append-only and written concurrently by two sharded Kaggle
# notebooks. Without this, two shards appending different runs at EOF produce a
# rebase conflict on every push -- and the natural "fix" under time pressure is to
# resolve it by picking one side, which silently discards a completed run and the
# GPU-hours that produced it.
#
# merge=union keeps BOTH sides' lines. That is correct precisely because the file
# is append-only and line-independent: order carries no meaning, and duplicate
# run_ids are de-duplicated on read (registry.load_runs).
results/runs.jsonl merge=union text eol=lf

# Selection artifacts are write-once. A conflict here means two machines produced
# different selections for the same (method, ratio, seed), which is a real problem
# that must NOT be auto-resolved -- it means the selection is not reproducible.
results/selections/*.json -merge

*.npy binary
